  "query_preview": "..\n\nIn the mid to upper levels, a trough sits over southern\nCalifornia and northern Baja today, while southerly flow in the\nlower levels will draw moisture northward into the desert\nsouthwest. By this afternoon precipitable water values will \nhave risen to 1.2-1.45 inches from central Pima eastward while \nsurface dew points will be mainly in the 50s. Early morning \nsatellite imagery shows an expansive moving into the region out \nof northern Sonora and Chihuahua, which may keep instability on \nthe low end today depending on its coverage over southeastern \nArizona. Rain chances of 30-70 percent are fairly widespread \ntoday from central Pima through the New Mexico border as the \nnearby trough will kick off showers and thunderstorms mainly \nthis afternoon and evening. If there is a threat for heavy \nrainfall rates and potentially flash flooding, it will likely \nneed to come from areas that see some sun and surface heating \ntoday to drive stronger thunderstorm chances. The 00Z HREF \nshows a potential for heavy rainfall rates on the western end \nthis cirrus shield from Santa Cruz and western Cochise counties \ninto the Tucson metro, though again this will be highly \ndependent on adequate insolation. Otherwise further east longer\ndurations of moderate rainfall rates may also produce flooding \nconcerns, but this threat will likely need multiple rounds of \nstorms cells passing over a specific area. On the river flooding\nside, the Gila River will need to be monitored out of New \nMexico with the heavy rainfall potential out east today.\n\nScattered rain showers may continue through the night, \nespecially through Cochise, Graham, and Greenlee counties, as a \nshortwave impulse will kick out of the parent trough late \ntonight through Wednesday. Shower and thunderstorm chances \ncontinue on Wednesday with the lingering upper level support, \nthough timing is still uncertain if early morning activity lasts\nlonger into the day additional development may be limited. \nThursday comes with the same story as the main trough should \nbegin to move through and eventually exit the area, but with \nuncertain instability and precipitation coverage. Chances are \nmainly kept to areas near the New Mexico border on Friday with \nthe trough's exit and Saturday has a mainly dry forecast. Low \nend chances return Sunday into early next week as a shortwave \ntrough should cross to the north. Differences in timing and \ntrough amplitude keep these chances in the 10-30 percent range \neach day for now. \n\nThe eastern Pacific remains active this week with Polo now a\nhurricane off the southwest Mexico coast. Polo is forecast to\nremain well to the south into the weekend, with uncertainty in\nits path or even approach towards the southwest United States beyond\nthat.",
